The way to do this is to have a main loop that handles the current dialogue. For example:
Dialogue currentDialogue = dialogues.get(0);

while (true) {
    System.out.println(currentDialogue.getText());

    for (int i = 0; i < currentDialogue.getOptions().size(); i++) {
        System.out.println(i + 1 + ". " + currentDialogue.getOptions().get(i).getText());
    }

    int choice = getUserInput(); // this is a function that gets the user's choice

    if (choice < 1 || choice > currentDialogue.getOptions().size()) {
        System.out.println("Invalid choice. Please try again.");
        continue;
    }

    String nextDialogueId = currentDialogue.getOptions().get(choice - 1).getNextDialogueId();
    currentDialogue = dialogues.get(nextDialogueId);
}
In this example, the dialogues are stored in a Map<String, Dialogue> where the key is the dialogue ID. The getUserInput() function is a placeholder for whatever method you use to get the user's input. The loop continues until the user makes a valid choice, and then the currentDialogue is updated to the next dialogue based on the selected option.
